--- body ---
## Intent (author decisions, 2026-10-15)
`scripts/zotero.py` becomes local-only and stateless:

- Remove every Zotero Web API path (item reads and writes, file upload,
  enrichment writes), the API keys and their credential plumbing, the
  library index cache and its sync subcommand, and the injection record kept
  between runs. No local index, no cache, no record.
- Reads: `zotero.sqlite` opened read-only on every call; dedup and matching
  run against it. (Superseded on 2026-10-16, below: reads go through the
  local API.)
- Creating items with PDFs goes through the Zotero desktop connector
  (`127.0.0.1:23119/connector/`). Zotero must be running; fail loudly when the
  connector does not answer. Never write `zotero.sqlite`.
- `enrich` and `reconcile --apply` cannot edit existing items: they report
  the suggested edits (item key, field, old → new) for the author.
- Ticket 1111 is superseded; what still applies is kept and 1111 closes in
  the same PR.
- Skill, references, `docs/zotero-integration.md`, `rules/zotero.md` and
  `README.md` carry no Web API, key or index-cache instruction. The
  "Periodic sync + purge" section of `rules/zotero.md` belongs to ticket 1112.

## Author decisions, 2026-10-16 (binding, verbatim)
1. "API ONLY, NEVER SQLITE." All library reads go through the Zotero 7 local
   API (http://127.0.0.1:23119/api/, e.g. /api/users/0/items, groups via
   /api/groups/<id>/...; read-only, no key). Remove every zotero.sqlite read
   path: zotero_open, mode=ro/immutable fallback, read_mode, WAL refusal,
   database discovery (ZOTERO_DATA_DIR, profile parsing), the
   --out-names-the-database guards that only exist because of the db. The
   duplicate check is then always live. Find out from Zotero's source/docs
   how the local API exposes attachment file paths (needed by
   attach/audit/reconcile) and the "allow other applications to communicate
   with Zotero" setting; when the local API is disabled or answers
   unexpectedly, fail with a clear message naming that setting. Still
   stateless: no index, no cache, no ledger.
2. Zotero not up: "If Zotero is not up, try launching it, and if you can't,
   do nothing. Containers do nothing." So: when the connector/local API does
   not answer, and we are NOT in a container, try to launch the desktop
   Zotero (detached; find the binary on PATH / common install / flatpak) and
   wait for it to answer within a stated bound. If it does not come up, do
   nothing: no partial action, a clear message, a report status saying
   Zotero was unavailable. In a container (detect /.dockerenv,
   /run/.containerenv, cgroup hints, CI env, no display), never try to
   launch: do nothing the same way. Choose exit codes so an unattended
   reconcile treats "unavailable" as a clean no-op, not a crash, and
   document the choice.

## Original context
2026-10-15, host doudou: `python3 scripts/zotero.py reconcile .` in report mode
on the harness, with a library index cache older than 24 h (4.7 MB, written
2026-10-14 15:21), was killed by `timeout 300` with no report. The time went
into the Web API rebuild of that cache. Removing the cache removes the cost: a
full read of the local library takes about 0.4 s (7,672 works, 9,451
attachments, measured on doudou 2026-10-15).

## Exit criteria
- [ ] No Web API call, key, index cache or injection record remains live in
      scripts/, skills/, rules/, docs/ or README.md
- [ ] Reads go through the local API, live, per call; no zotero.sqlite read
      path remains; a disabled or unexpected local API fails naming the
      setting (restated 2026-10-16; was: sqlite read-only with an
      immutable fallback and a read mode in every output)
- [ ] Zotero down: launched (never in a container) within a stated bound,
      else the command does nothing and reports Zotero unavailable;
      `reconcile` then exits 0
- [ ] `inject` creates items and PDFs through the desktop connector and
      does nothing, saying so, when Zotero is unavailable; tests use a
      local stub
- [ ] `enrich`, `attach` and `reconcile --apply` report edits to existing
      items instead of writing them
- [ ] `make check` passes
